// wdoc section: Formatting (text & inline patterns, headings, code).
page wdoc_formatting { sites = [:reference]
  h1 "Formatting"

  p "The everyday building blocks of a page: prose paragraphs with inline formatting, the six heading levels, and syntax-highlighted code listings."

  h2 "Paragraphs with p"

  p "`p \"...\"` is the canonical paragraph shorthand. The label text is the paragraph's content, and inline patterns light up automatically."

  code wcl {
    source = <<'WCL'
p "Quick prose with **bold** and `inline code`."
p "A whole paragraph in one line."
WCL
  }

  h2 "Inline patterns"

  p "The following patterns are recognised in any `p` body, in spans inside a `text` block, and in `utf8` table cells:"
  code wcl {
    source = <<'WCL'
**bold**             // → bold class
_italic_             // → italic class
`code`               // → monospace inline code
[text](page)         // → in-site link to a page
[text](site:page)    // → cross-site link
[text](https://...)  // → external link
:lucide.check:       // → inline icon (pack.name)
$x^2$                // → inline LaTeX (text style)
$$\int x \, dx$$     // → display-style LaTeX
WCL
  }
  
  h2 "Headings"

  p "Six heading levels are available — `h1` through `h6` — each taking the heading text as a single inline label and an optional `id`."

  code wcl {
    source = <<'WCL'
h1 "Title"             { id = top }
h2 "Section"
h3 "Subsection"
h4 "Details"
h5 "Fine points"
h6 "Minutiae"
WCL
  }
  
  h2 "Code blocks"

  p "`code <lang> { source = ... }` renders a syntax-highlighted listing. The language tag picks the grammar; an unknown tag falls back to plain text."

  code wcl {
    source = <<'WCL'
code rust {
  source = <<'RUST'
fn fib(n: u32) -> u64 {
    match n {
        0 => 0,
        1 => 1,
        _ => fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2),
    }
}
RUST
}
WCL
  }

  p "wdoc highlights a wide range of languages (via syntect + two-face). The tag is the language name or a common file extension; WCL ships its own grammar, so `code wcl { … }` highlights too. Supported tags include:"

  column {
    widths = [33.3, 33.3, 33.3]
    list {
      li "`rust`"
      li "`python`"
      li "`javascript`"
      li "`typescript`"
      li "`go`"
      li "`c`"
      li "`cpp`"
      li "`java`"
      li "`kotlin`"
    }
    list {
      li "`ruby`"
      li "`php`"
      li "`swift`"
      li "`html`"
      li "`css`"
      li "`scss`"
      li "`json`"
      li "`yaml`"
      li "`toml`"
    }
    list {
      li "`xml`"
      li "`markdown`"
      li "`sql`"
      li "`bash`"
      li "`dockerfile`"
      li "`lua`"
      li "`haskell`"
      li "`scala`"
      li "`wcl`"
    }
  }

  p "The `source` is usually a heredoc — use a raw heredoc (`<<'TAG'`) so the body is taken verbatim, with no escapes or interpolation to mangle backslash-heavy code. See [Strings & Heredocs](strings) for the full syntax."

  h2 "Lists"

  p "A `list` holds `li` items. It's a bullet list by default; set `style = :numbered` for a numbered one. Each `li`'s text runs through the inline-pattern engine, so **bold**, `code`, links, and icons all work inside items."

  code wcl {
    source = <<'WCL'
list {
  li "Plain item"
  li "With **bold** and a [link](wdoc_overview)"
}

list { style = :numbered
  li "First step"
  li "Second step"
}
WCL
  }

  p "Nest an `li` inside an `li` for a sublist — it inherits the parent list's style, so a numbered list numbers sublists hierarchically (`2.1`, `2.2`). For a sublist with a *different* style, drop a whole `list` block inside the `li` instead."

  code wcl {
    source = <<'WCL'
list { style = :numbered
  li "Setup"
  li "Build" {
    li "Compile"          // renders 2.1
    li "Link"             // renders 2.2
  }
  li "Run" {
    list {                // a bulleted sublist inside a numbered list
      li "Foreground"
      li "Background"
    }
  }
}
WCL
  }

  h2 "Callouts"

  p "A `callout` is an admonition: an icon, a coloured heading, and a body. Six built-in types are selected by `class`, each shipping a default colour and icon."

  code wcl {
    source = <<'WCL'
callout "Note" {
  class = ["note"]
  body  = "Background context the reader should remember."
}

callout "Heads up" {
  class = ["warning"]
  body  = "Something to be careful about."
}

callout "All done" {
  class = ["success"]
  icon  = "lucide.party-popper"        // override the default icon
  body  = "Confirm an action completed."
}
WCL
  }

  p "The built-in types and their default colour + icon:"

  table {
    rows:
      | "Type"      | "Default colour" | "Default icon" |
      | "`note`"    | "Blue"           | "lucide.info" |
      | "`info`"    | "Blue"           | "lucide.info" |
      | "`tip`"     | "Cyan"           | "lucide.lightbulb" |
      | "`warning`" | "Orange"         | "lucide.triangle-alert" |
      | "`error`"   | "Red"            | "lucide.circle-x" |
      | "`success`" | "Green"          | "lucide.circle-check" |
  }

  p "Its fields:"

  table {
    rows:
      | "Field"   | "Meaning" |
      | "`<heading>`" | "Inline label — the coloured heading at the top of the callout." |
      | "`body`"  | "The prose under the heading. Plain text — see the warning below." |
      | "`class`" | "Selects the type: `[\"note\"]` / `[\"tip\"]` / etc. May also carry user classes." |
      | "`icon`"  | "Override the default icon (any `pack.name` from a built-in or declared iconset)." |
      | "`id`"    | "Optional explicit HTML id." |
  }

  p "Next: [Math Equations](wdoc_math)."
}
